hat can be built against the individual  that they participate in atrocities or  they committed atrocities while they  were fighting alongside Islamic state  due process will kick in and they will  usually go to jail even going to live in  Isis territory is a crime under EU law  locking up returning fighters  demonstrates a tough stance from  government and reassures the public but  prison poses its own risks many  jihadists have previously been in jail  before committing attacks the  government's own officially appointed  inspector into the problem of Prison  radicalization and said that the problem  with out of control that the prisons are  jihadist training run at the moment  convicted terrorists should be behind  bars but not all returning fighters fall  into this category whose sama hasan took  part in jihad in the 1990s when soviet  forces invaded afghanistan i'm a former  foreign fighter and a portent of terror  started when i was 19 and of course that  was all legal in those days and they  loaded order here around the arab world  i was willing to take up arms and fight  and die for a noble cause and that's  what i think we have to recognize and  understand is a lot of young men and  women to become the terrorists are  motivated by noble ideals but noble  causes many foreign fighters travel to  syria long before Isis emerged and the  brutality and the headings began they  went to defend fellow Muslims in the  uprising against President Bashar  al-assad  others went to live in the newly  declared Islamic state in search of a  more pious life  there are a lot of people who thought  that going to Isis is they're going to  building you a table in society very  Mironova spent seven months embedded  with the iraqi special operation forces  in mosul trying to understand what makes  Isis fighters tick I was able to visit  Isis houses where they need foreign  fighters their basis to see what books  they read how did they leave and there's  like very interesting things were  finding like love letters and cooking  recipes it's very important to show and  to understand that people in Isis or any  other group they're normal people many  who went to live under Isis have seen  and done terrible things those who felt  the Caliphate did not live up to the  utopian propaganda wanted out but faced  imprisonment if they went home  we hear people say you know lock them up  and throw away the key  the whole point of our society similar  society is to give people a second  chance and give them a chance of  rehabilitation  not many governments are willing to  offer returning as a second chance but  in Denmark they're giving it a try  instead of prison sentences some get  counseling and help finding jobs and  housing the aim is to combat the root  cause of terrorism  there are a group of people in every  country basically that if you eliminated  that they feel that everyone is against  them that they are basically very  unhappy with the whole situation and  then a group comes and this puts an arm  around them and says oh come on come to  us we're gonna help you with Isis almost  defeated on the ground it may seek to  step up terrorist attacks around the  world  [Music]  the Danish authorities hope that former  fighters reintegrated back into society  will feel less alienated and  disillusioned traits that Isis  recruiters prey upon  but critics cite and lack of hard  evidence that the Danish model is  working and rehabilitation is a hard  policy to sell I think politically it  will be very difficult for the  government in a Western European country  to take individual who's been fighting  alongside a proscribed terrorist  organization like Isis or al-qaeda that  has made an avowed intent to launch  attacks on our country and for the  government to spend a considerable  amount of money basically giving this  person a life it only takes one or two  determined individuals to carry out an  atrocity so close surveillance of  returning fighters is essential even  those in prison may eventually be  released but keeping a close watch on  just one person long-term takes a lot of  manpower so governments face the  difficult decision  of whom to watch and whom to ignore  you

